WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:05.000
Out of all of the streaming services, Disney Plus&nbsp;
has had one of the most rocky roads from launch&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.000 --> 00:00:09.080
and it turns out that the behind the scenes of&nbsp;
the platform is a lot more dire than we could have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:09.080 --> 00:00:13.760
imagined. I’ve been gearing up to do an overall&nbsp;
Disney Plus breakdown video, but the thing that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:13.760 --> 00:00:18.680
really springboarded this into existence is a&nbsp;
new article released by The Hollywood Reporter&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.680 --> 00:00:22.960
that sheds light on a lot of major issues&nbsp;
behind the scenes that have been negatively&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.960 --> 00:00:28.760
impacting MCU shows. So I’ll be covering a lot of&nbsp;
information revealed from this article while also&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:28.760 --> 00:00:33.720
rounding up numerous other articles that go&nbsp;
into the controversies behind the scenes. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:33.720 --> 00:00:38.760
I’ll link all this information into general big&nbsp;
issues with Disney Plus’ content strategy for the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:38.760 --> 00:00:44.560
MCU and Star Wars. So the first and most damning&nbsp;
revelation brought up by The Hollywood Reporter&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:44.560 --> 00:00:50.120
is that none of the MCU shows have had actual&nbsp;
showrunners. First, I should probably describe the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:50.120 --> 00:00:54.600
role of a showrunner because it can get a little&nbsp;
confusing as to who’s in control of the creative&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:54.600 --> 00:01:00.160
vision given so many positions like directors,&nbsp;
writers, producers, and executive producers that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.160 --> 00:01:05.120
have a say in how a show is made. The showrunner&nbsp;
essentially has full creative authority over a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:05.120 --> 00:01:10.840
series and manages every aspect about a show from&nbsp;
the writing, filming, and editing. And it’s up to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:10.840 --> 00:01:15.880
them if they want to personally be involved with&nbsp;
writing and directing certain episodes. Producers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:15.880 --> 00:01:20.600
and executive producers are technically higher on&nbsp;
the food chain, but their creative input entails&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:20.600 --> 00:01:25.040
finding people to work on a project and supervise&nbsp;
the process to see if everything is running&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:25.040 --> 00:01:30.960
smoothly and is also on budget. However, Marvel on&nbsp;
the other hand didn’t even bother with acquiring&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:30.960 --> 00:01:35.560
showrunners. They generally approached directors&nbsp;
and asked them to pitch shows and they were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:35.560 --> 00:01:40.200
normally not heavily involved with the writing&nbsp;
process. The primary director of Moon Knight,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:40.200 --> 00:01:45.400
Mohamed Diab, said he was “approached out of the&nbsp;
blue” by Marvel and asked for him to pitch the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:45.400 --> 00:01:50.120
series. From here, they didn’t actually make him&nbsp;
the showrunner and brought on more creatives to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:50.120 --> 00:01:55.680
help lighten the workload. Luckily, Moon Knight is&nbsp;
an example of this actually working to a degree,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:55.680 --> 00:02:01.440
but a bad example where this was a similar case&nbsp;
was Secret Invasion. Now it was largely a mystery&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:01.440 --> 00:02:06.200
as to why Secret Invasion was so expensive and&nbsp;
looked the least remarkable and most cheap of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.200 --> 00:02:11.800
the MCU shows. But it turns out that the show&nbsp;
was utterly plagued with production issues and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:11.800 --> 00:02:17.040
had a staggering four months of reshoots.&nbsp;
Along with the producers bringing on a new&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:17.040 --> 00:02:21.560
writer during that period to write new material.&nbsp;
So they essentially re-made a solid chunk of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:21.560 --> 00:02:26.640
Secret Invasion in that time. To go into more&nbsp;
detail behind the scenes for Secret Invasion,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:26.640 --> 00:02:31.120
the worrying signs started with Kyle Bradstreet&nbsp;
writing scripts for the show for almost a year,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:31.120 --> 00:02:35.640
until Marvel spontaneously decided that they&nbsp;
wanted to take the show in a different direction&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:35.640 --> 00:02:40.280
and drop kicked him out of the series. They hired&nbsp;
a few directors and a new writer and allegedly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.280 --> 00:02:45.120
none of them got along during pre-production. It&nbsp;
was an absolute shitstorm as this unproductive&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:45.120 --> 00:02:49.800
crew worked inefficiently and by a certain point,&nbsp;
they were burning through their allotted schedule&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:49.800 --> 00:02:54.960
to the point that they lost one of the directors&nbsp;
due to scheduling conflicts, forcing Ali Selim to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:54.960 --> 00:03:00.600
now direct every episode. On top of most of the&nbsp;
people working on the show getting replaced.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:00.600 --> 00:03:05.840
The Marvel executive who was overseeing Secret&nbsp;
Invasion upsetted the company so much to the point&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:05.840 --> 00:03:11.320
that his contract will not be getting renewed. So&nbsp;
Secret Invasion was an absolute nightmare behind&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:11.320 --> 00:03:16.240
the scenes and I think is one of the straws that&nbsp;
broke the camel's back for Marvel in realizing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:16.240 --> 00:03:21.520
that this model is not sustainable. Along with it&nbsp;
fundamentally delivering a worse product because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:21.520 --> 00:03:25.960
by having so many conflicting creative opinions,&nbsp;
the show will feel like a mess as everyone tries&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:25.960 --> 00:03:30.960
to get on the same page. Secret Invasion was&nbsp;
this hideous Frankenstein baby that failed to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:30.960 --> 00:03:36.400
deliver on the premise of the show and was this&nbsp;
weird mash-up of crappy spy espionage, half-assed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:36.400 --> 00:03:41.240
paranoia, and so much more that I dive into for&nbsp;
a twenty-minute video I’ll put in the end screen&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:41.240 --> 00:03:49.680
if you’re interested. Then there’s also the issue&nbsp;
that Disney didn’t hire on more Marvel executives&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:49.680 --> 00:03:55.240
to oversee TV shows that suddenly landed on&nbsp;
their doorsteps because of Disney Plus. Instead,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:55.240 --> 00:03:59.720
the existing executives working for Marvel had&nbsp;
to split their time between the movies and the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:59.720 --> 00:04:04.480
shows. Which this piece of information to me is&nbsp;
a major reason why Marvel has quickly been going&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:04.480 --> 00:04:09.680
off a cliff in terms of quality. They essentially&nbsp;
doubled their output of Marvel content over night&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:09.680 --> 00:04:13.920
and they were clearly not ready to handle the&nbsp;
massive amount of work. Because before this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:13.920 --> 00:04:18.360
they had 2 to 3 movies a year they had to worry&nbsp;
about, along with those movies just being around&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:18.360 --> 00:04:23.200
two hours. So actually, they more than tripled&nbsp;
the amount of content they had to worry about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:23.200 --> 00:04:28.360
because the shows generally run for around 4 to&nbsp;
5 hours. Like you’d probably be surprised if I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:28.360 --> 00:04:34.640
told you in 2021 Disney Plus released five MCU&nbsp;
shows. At a certain point there must have been&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:34.640 --> 00:04:40.320
well over a dozen Marvel projects being worked on&nbsp;
at a single time. From here, Kevin Fiege probably&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:40.320 --> 00:04:44.720
couldn’t be as involved as he would want to be&nbsp;
with most of these projects. It seems like he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:44.720 --> 00:04:50.320
put his faith in the creatives, despite there not&nbsp;
being showrunners. So scripts clearly deteriorated&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:50.320 --> 00:04:55.800
and there have been a plethora of writing problems&nbsp;
on almost every MCU project coming up, especially&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:55.800 --> 00:05:01.160
Blade which seems to be in a perpetual hell of&nbsp;
writing. Now the proof that points to Kevin Fiege&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:01.160 --> 00:05:06.400
most likely not having enough supervision over MCU&nbsp;
content is what just happened with Daredevil Born&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:06.400 --> 00:05:11.440
Again. This event was the major talking point&nbsp;
of the Hollywood Reporter article. It reveals&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:11.440 --> 00:05:16.040
Marvel firing all the writers and directors on the&nbsp;
series because Fiege disliked the direction and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:16.040 --> 00:05:21.440
quality of the show. Funnily enough, the writers&nbsp;
strike actually gave Fiege some breathing room&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:21.440 --> 00:05:25.360
and allowed him to fully review the first&nbsp;
4 episodes that were shot before production&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:25.360 --> 00:05:29.760
shut down. And since he didn’t like what he saw,&nbsp;
which apparently Matt Murdoch doesn’t actually put&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:29.760 --> 00:05:34.720
on his suit until episode 4 and was just a legal&nbsp;
drama basically, he hit the reset button on the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:34.720 --> 00:05:40.040
show. So thankfully this strike led to more great&nbsp;
things besides giving writers significantly better&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:40.040 --> 00:05:46.160
contracts. The strike saved a fan-favorite show&nbsp;
from possibly another Secret Invasion esque fate.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:46.160 --> 00:05:50.440
Without the strike it’s likely that this show&nbsp;
would have continued shooting without a gauruntee&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:50.440 --> 00:05:55.400
as to whether it would’ve been good. Generally in&nbsp;
television you would make a pilot episode. Which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:55.400 --> 00:05:59.840
would act as the first episode of the series that&nbsp;
you would then show to the producers to greenlight&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:59.840 --> 00:06:04.280
the rest of the season. Or if they didn’t&nbsp;
like the pilot, they’d save money and say no&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:04.280 --> 00:06:09.120
by not continuing production on the entire show.&nbsp;
Because it’s fundamentally better to lose money&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:09.120 --> 00:06:15.440
on 1 episode instead of funding a multi-hour long&nbsp;
failure. Marvel however didn’t do this and just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:15.440 --> 00:06:19.560
greenlighted shows on the faith that the creatives&nbsp;
were hopefully competent enough to work on the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:19.560 --> 00:06:24.440
project, the scripts were finished and passable,&nbsp;
and in general they started floating this notion&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:24.440 --> 00:06:30.000
that any issues they’d run into in production they&nbsp;
could fix in post. Which is an utter nightmare&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:30.000 --> 00:06:34.600
for anyone working in the industry. It leads&nbsp;
to a lazy form of filmmaking with not enough&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:34.600 --> 00:06:40.080
planning being done and it also sends VFX artists&nbsp;
into a frenzy because inexperienced directors are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:40.080 --> 00:06:45.520
constantly making changes on already finished&nbsp;
shots with VFX. Or producers are finally seeing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:45.520 --> 00:06:50.960
the finished product and are now throwing in their&nbsp;
own notes for changes. Thus adding to the budget&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:50.960 --> 00:06:56.440
and forcing those artists to crunch even more.&nbsp;
When people bash bad VFX in Marvel movies and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:56.440 --> 00:07:01.280
is this major reason as to why these movies or&nbsp;
shows are bad, I just feel disconnected by the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:01.280 --> 00:07:06.000
conversation because the artists making these&nbsp;
shots are actually very talented and are being&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:06.000 --> 00:07:11.520
rused to finish their work. Most people will&nbsp;
always point out the bad VFX, but to be fair&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:11.520 --> 00:07:16.360
they do deliver some genuinely great shots that&nbsp;
look incredible at times. It just unfortunately&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:16.360 --> 00:07:21.280
breaks your immersion when those occasional bad&nbsp;
shots do arise. Like it’s been reported that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:21.280 --> 00:07:26.120
Marvel productions are regularly making changes&nbsp;
on their projects right up until the deadline.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:26.120 --> 00:07:30.640
Which just seems insane to me because with proper&nbsp;
planning and pre-production that should never be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:30.640 --> 00:07:35.240
the case. Your movie or show should be perfectly&nbsp;
planned out to the point that you don’t drive up&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:35.240 --> 00:07:40.560
the budget of the show while simultaneously&nbsp;
hurting quality. However, this takes time and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:40.560 --> 00:07:45.520
since Marvel Studios essentially had a quota of&nbsp;
about 3 shows a year, they didn’t have the luxury&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:45.520 --> 00:07:50.880
of time so therefore the quality started to tank.&nbsp;
Even though it seems counterproductive to have a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:50.880 --> 00:07:56.280
lack of planning and then go through months of&nbsp;
reshoots. All of this amalgamates into Marvel&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:56.280 --> 00:08:00.560
feeling more like a conveyer belt of content&nbsp;
instead of something that is more driven by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:00.560 --> 00:08:05.440
creativity and passion. We do occasionally&nbsp;
get those shows like Loki and Moon Knight,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:05.440 --> 00:08:10.360
but other shows like Wandavision which were good,&nbsp;
clearly had some really inconsistent patches like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:10.360 --> 00:08:15.880
the ending that just felt like a complete mess.&nbsp;
Or the obvious elephant in the room that is Secret&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:15.880 --> 00:08:21.160
Invasion in which Marvel just tossed 200 million&nbsp;
dollars off to a group of creatives in the hopes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:21.160 --> 00:08:26.080
that they’d return with a good show. So since&nbsp;
we’ve essentially assessed the absolute chaos&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:26.080 --> 00:08:30.360
behind the scenes at Disney Plus and why so&nbsp;
much of their content has been lacking, let’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:30.360 --> 00:08:35.840
now see how this impacts the streaming service&nbsp;
as a whole and the major issues that creates.

00:08:35.840 --> 00:08:40.240
Going into the biggest problem is that for&nbsp;
both Marvel and Star Wars, Disney is flooding&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:40.240 --> 00:08:45.520
the market with so much content to the point that&nbsp;
they are oversaturating and damaging their brand.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:45.520 --> 00:08:49.800
Now this is a very nuanced subject that I’ll&nbsp;
elaborate on and there is definitely another&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:49.800 --> 00:08:53.960
side to this coin. And that side is what&nbsp;
James Gunn brings up in that people aren’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:53.960 --> 00:08:59.000
getting superhero fatigue and instead they are&nbsp;
getting bad writing or bad story fatigue. For me,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:59.000 --> 00:09:03.440
I pretty much agree with this sentiment because&nbsp;
if we kept getting S-tier Marvel content like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:03.440 --> 00:09:08.880
Winter Soldier, Thor: Ragnarok, and Guardians of&nbsp;
the Galaxy then people would be perfectly happy.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:08.880 --> 00:09:12.960
Those movies were all refreshing and told&nbsp;
great stories that were imbued with so much&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:12.960 --> 00:09:18.160
creativity and love. And their follow-ups were all&nbsp;
generally pretty good and I feel like Guardians of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:18.160 --> 00:09:23.640
the Galaxy 3 is living proof that people aren’t&nbsp;
fatigued by overall superhero movies. Like if I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:23.640 --> 00:09:28.880
were to personally rank phase 4 it would look&nbsp;
something like this, and in comparison this is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:28.880 --> 00:09:34.440
how I would rank the first 3 phases. Which is a&nbsp;
pretty depressing contrast. People are much more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:34.440 --> 00:09:39.560
aware to the deteriorating quality of the content&nbsp;
to the point that reviews now make or break Marvel&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:39.560 --> 00:09:44.720
movies. That’s why Ant-Man Quantumania did poorly&nbsp;
at the box-office because critics and fans ripped&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:44.720 --> 00:09:49.200
that movie apart, leaving people on the fence&nbsp;
to either skip it entirely or wait until it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:49.200 --> 00:09:54.200
comes out on Disney Plus. Despite Quantumania&nbsp;
having some hype marketing in that they were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:54.200 --> 00:09:59.120
introducing Kang as the new big threat. So&nbsp;
this waning quality paired with a drastic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:59.120 --> 00:10:04.120
uptick in content on Disney Plus is what’s&nbsp;
driving the community to this burnt out view.

00:10:04.120 --> 00:10:08.880
Then another aspect for the MCU in particular is&nbsp;
that the universe is exponentially expanding to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:08.880 --> 00:10:14.080
an alarming degree with so many new characters and&nbsp;
plot threads to the point that it’s overwhelming.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:14.080 --> 00:10:18.800
For every show they seemingly create more loose&nbsp;
ends than they do for plotlines they conclude. To&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:18.800 --> 00:10:24.280
go through some examples, WandaVision establishes&nbsp;
4 separate plotlines, some of which have no clear&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:24.280 --> 00:10:29.240
plan for the future like white vision being set&nbsp;
loose into the world, Hawkeye oddly sets up a lot&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:29.240 --> 00:10:33.680
of different plotlines, new characters some times&nbsp;
show up out of the blue like in She-Hulk where&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:33.680 --> 00:10:38.400
Hulk randomly has a son now, Secret Invasion&nbsp;
introduces the most overpowered character in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:38.400 --> 00:10:43.040
the MCU who will show up at some other point&nbsp;
I guess, and even shows that are meant to be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:43.040 --> 00:10:48.160
a mini-series end up concluding on a cliffhanger.&nbsp;
Actually looking back at Moon Knight I completely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:48.160 --> 00:10:52.320
forgot that this character became a superhero.&nbsp;
Then this isn’t even bringing into account the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:52.320 --> 00:10:56.960
usual end credit scene introductions for the&nbsp;
movies. At this point it feels like cutting off&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:56.960 --> 00:11:02.320
the head of a hydra in which when one story ends&nbsp;
two more take it’s place. On top of all of this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:02.320 --> 00:11:07.600
being compounded by Disney Plus adding more wood&nbsp;
to the fire. This does vary per person because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:07.600 --> 00:11:13.880
fans who watch every show or movie may not care as&nbsp;
much, but for casuals it’s really uninviting. You&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:13.880 --> 00:11:18.960
now have to ingest dozens of hours of content just&nbsp;
to understand where these characters came from.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:18.960 --> 00:11:24.120
The Marvels as an example features two characters&nbsp;
that originated in two separate shows each over 4&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:24.120 --> 00:11:29.560
hours long, so to understand the complexities and&nbsp;
context for these characters, you need to watch&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:29.560 --> 00:11:34.680
their shows. Or you have a situation like Doctor&nbsp;
Strange and the Multiverse of Madness where fans&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:34.680 --> 00:11:39.560
who didn’t watch WandaVision were confused about&nbsp;
why Wanda was suddenly a villain. The Marvels&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:39.560 --> 00:11:44.040
may not require you to watch Ms. Marvel and&nbsp;
WandaVision to understand these new characters,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:44.040 --> 00:11:49.280
because they’ll probably re-introduce them in a&nbsp;
new way that’s digestible for casual moviegoers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:49.280 --> 00:11:53.000
but that notion will always be in the back of&nbsp;
your head that you are missing a part of their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:53.000 --> 00:11:57.760
story. Because at the end of the day, you miss an&nbsp;
entire arc that these characters went on in their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:57.760 --> 00:12:03.120
own shows. This state in comparison to the first&nbsp;
few phases were much more casual viewer-friendly.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:03.120 --> 00:12:07.960
All we had were a small handful of characters that&nbsp;
took on conflict that wasn’t too overwhelming from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:07.960 --> 00:12:12.960
an overarching story perspective. The dynamics&nbsp;
between these characters had a significant amount&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:12.960 --> 00:12:18.160
of depth and were all able to be explored to&nbsp;
a good extent. Like I’d honestly be surprised&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:18.160 --> 00:12:22.920
if we ever get a deeply personal moment again&nbsp;
where Tony Stark finds out that Bucky killed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:22.920 --> 00:12:27.160
his parents. I know we're primarily in a phase&nbsp;
where we're setting things up again, but with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:27.160 --> 00:12:31.480
so many more characters it’s hard to imagine&nbsp;
them getting back to that level of depth that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:31.480 --> 00:12:36.680
we had between Iron Man and Captain America. Now,&nbsp;
I just shutter at the thought of anyone trying to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:36.680 --> 00:12:41.960
write or direct the next Avengers movie because&nbsp;
the amount of characters has more than doubled.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:41.960 --> 00:12:46.240
Infinity War did an excellent job balancing&nbsp;
out its cast of characters, but the question&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:46.240 --> 00:12:50.320
is will that still work for the next Avengers&nbsp;
movie if we bring in every single superhero&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:50.320 --> 00:12:54.520
we’ve been setting up. There’s almost no way&nbsp;
we can get back to that level of detail from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:54.520 --> 00:12:58.400
the earlier phases because by doing that,&nbsp;
you’ll have to cut out a lot of screen time&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:58.400 --> 00:13:03.440
for other heroes. Which begs the question, why&nbsp;
introduce so many if they aren’t going to be that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:03.440 --> 00:13:08.200
important later on? I know this is a comic book&nbsp;
thing where you have like a hundred characters,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:08.200 --> 00:13:12.800
but I’d argue that’s more unsustainable in a&nbsp;
movie or show setting. People tend to want quality&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:12.800 --> 00:13:18.440
over quantity and the current track record for&nbsp;
Disney Plus has shown us that we can’t have both.

00:13:18.440 --> 00:13:22.360
Taking a look over at Star Wars, there are&nbsp;
obviously many issues for each project that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:22.360 --> 00:13:26.040
generally have to do with the writing, but I&nbsp;
want to take a look at a larger problem with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:26.040 --> 00:13:31.680
their shows on Disney Plus. And that’s the lack&nbsp;
of creative and new ideas for the franchise. Now&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:31.680 --> 00:13:36.320
there are very few like the Mandalorian, but even&nbsp;
that show has devolved into even bringing back&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:36.320 --> 00:13:41.640
likeable characters that in turn deteriorate their&nbsp;
strong payoff earlier in the story. Every single&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:41.640 --> 00:13:46.600
show essentially takes place within one time&nbsp;
period of the universe because that is where these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:46.600 --> 00:13:52.440
recognizable characters are, and thus, more people&nbsp;
will pay to watch it. That’s just the sad truth&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:52.440 --> 00:13:56.480
because there’s nothing that will inherently draw&nbsp;
in tons of viewers right away if a show doesn’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:56.480 --> 00:14:02.320
have major recognizable characters in them. Like&nbsp;
Ahsoka as an example included Anakin Skywalker and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:02.320 --> 00:14:08.240
the entire fandom lost their collective minds and&nbsp;
thought it was the best thing ever. So when Disney&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:08.240 --> 00:14:13.240
is at a crossroads of making original stories with&nbsp;
new characters or mining the nostalgia of fans&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:13.240 --> 00:14:18.440
with bringing back existing popular characters,&nbsp;
you can probably guess which path they will take.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:18.440 --> 00:14:23.000
It more or less stifles creativity when you force&nbsp;
future stories through a bottleneck when there are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:23.000 --> 00:14:27.480
so many other eras within the universe you can&nbsp;
explore. Disney has said that they are planning&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:27.480 --> 00:14:32.320
on doing that, but given that they are cutting&nbsp;
back on producing more shows it’s unclear how&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:32.320 --> 00:14:36.720
many we are really going to get given that Dave&nbsp;
Filoni and Jon Favreau are taking up a lot of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:36.720 --> 00:14:41.960
the real estate. The Acolyte is promising but&nbsp;
that seems to still be far away. Besides that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:41.960 --> 00:14:46.360
the current state of Star Wars has been extremely&nbsp;
rough and the only show that has been truly great&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:46.360 --> 00:14:51.880
is Andor. And sadly that show didn’t perform well&nbsp;
because it didn’t have an iconic character backing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:51.880 --> 00:14:56.520
it and it also probably hurts that people are&nbsp;
burnt out on the rebel storyline. Despite the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:56.520 --> 00:15:01.320
show genuinely having some outstanding dramatic&nbsp;
writing. So we're at a point where the other&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:01.320 --> 00:15:06.440
half of content on Disney Plus has people mostly&nbsp;
tired of it and desperately wanting something new&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:06.440 --> 00:15:11.840
and refreshing. Also, it’s not necessarily a bad&nbsp;
thing to tell stories of existing characters like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:11.840 --> 00:15:16.920
Obi-Wan. The issue however arises when someone&nbsp;
doesn’t have an original and great vision for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:16.920 --> 00:15:22.960
the series and Disney forces and rushes the show&nbsp;
into existence. Or if they do have a good vision,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:22.960 --> 00:15:27.680
Disney may step in and jerry rig it into a happy,&nbsp;
light hearted, mini-series when it was supposed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:27.680 --> 00:15:34.160
to be a darker movie akin to Logan. Which in turn&nbsp;
angers the fans who want to love your franchise.

00:15:34.160 --> 00:15:38.840
This is essentially going into the next point in&nbsp;
that a lot of mini-series on Disney Plus feel like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:38.840 --> 00:15:43.400
they would have been better suited to be movies.&nbsp;
At a certain point you have to ask yourself,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:43.400 --> 00:15:49.040
is it better to have a 150 to 200 million dollar&nbsp;
budget for 6 to 8 episodes that have unnecessary&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:49.040 --> 00:15:54.480
elements and a slow pace, or just condense it&nbsp;
down to a solid 2 hour plus movie that is of a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:54.480 --> 00:15:59.880
much higher quality. Because the production value&nbsp;
for a lot of these shows feels cheap at times and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:59.880 --> 00:16:05.560
that really comes through for any action scene. It&nbsp;
has the rushed and lazy TV show feel and this is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:05.560 --> 00:16:10.400
felt the most in Star Wars where lightsaber fights&nbsp;
are supposed to be elaborately crafted with great&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:10.400 --> 00:16:16.680
choreography, dynamic movements, awe-inspiring&nbsp;
settings, and more. But on a TV show schedule and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:16.680 --> 00:16:20.680
budget, the creators probably don’t have enough&nbsp;
time and rehearsal to get footage that looks&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:20.680 --> 00:16:26.400
great. So to partially get around this, the action&nbsp;
is in turn not as high effort. Leading to what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:26.400 --> 00:16:31.400
feels like an overall downgrade. Or narratively&nbsp;
a lot of these shows were initially being written&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:31.400 --> 00:16:37.040
as movies and then management busted through the&nbsp;
door and saw potential to up Disney Plus’ catalog.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:37.040 --> 00:16:42.120
Examples being Obi-Wan and Secret Invasion. Maybe&nbsp;
then Secret Invasion could actually afford to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:42.120 --> 00:16:46.880
include more characters within its story because&nbsp;
that kind of premise desperately needed more than&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:46.880 --> 00:16:52.400
just Rhodey and Everett Ross showing up. But the&nbsp;
reality is that these actors are very expensive&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:52.400 --> 00:16:57.120
so even though Secret Invasion has a large&nbsp;
budget, they sadly can’t afford a large roster of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:57.120 --> 00:17:02.480
characters that the MCU has. Which does seem kind&nbsp;
of counterproductive in that you’ve introduced&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:02.480 --> 00:17:07.480
and brought in so many characters, but having&nbsp;
multiple of them in your shows or movies would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:07.480 --> 00:17:12.400
be too expensive because their price tags are in&nbsp;
the millions. There are only a small handful of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:12.400 --> 00:17:17.800
shows like WandaVision, Loki, She-Hulk despite my&nbsp;
dislike for it, and Andor that feel like they were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:17.800 --> 00:17:23.760
specifically made for TV and if they were movies&nbsp;
it would only be a downgrade for their stories.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:23.760 --> 00:17:28.200
They earn their time because every episode is&nbsp;
extremely productive in terms of its narrative&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:28.200 --> 00:17:32.840
and how it grows their characters. They have wide&nbsp;
sprawling and fun stories instead of something&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:32.840 --> 00:17:38.360
that is stretched out to fit 6 episodes. Looking&nbsp;
at you Jon Favreau. The Book of Boba Fett could&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:38.360 --> 00:17:42.600
have easily just been a movie and maybe then&nbsp;
they’d actually try to make their action scenes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:42.600 --> 00:17:47.760
look half decent. This seems to primarily be the&nbsp;
management of who’s running these shows because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:47.760 --> 00:17:52.640
when you look over at HBO, shows like House&nbsp;
of the Dragon that have a similar budget feel&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:52.640 --> 00:17:57.760
significantly better and of a higher quality than&nbsp;
anything Disney Plus has to offer. I used to think&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:57.760 --> 00:18:02.240
that it was just the directors who were butchering&nbsp;
these shows when in reality the fault probably&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:02.240 --> 00:18:07.680
goes much higher and wider than that. Although&nbsp;
some directors are clearly worse than others.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:07.680 --> 00:18:12.800
Disney Plus does have their occasional gems, but&nbsp;
most of them seem to be isolated cases of Disney&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:12.800 --> 00:18:17.920
stumbling onto a passionate creator that genuinely&nbsp;
want to make something outstanding. Instead of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:17.920 --> 00:18:22.280
their usual current affairs where the studio comes&nbsp;
up with an idea and finds someone to make it who&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:22.280 --> 00:18:27.840
doesn’t have a story that’s deeply engaging. The&nbsp;
crazy thing is this was essentially the inception&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:27.840 --> 00:18:33.200
of The Force Awakens where Disney forced a sequel&nbsp;
to happen and the writers had no idea what to do&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:33.200 --> 00:18:37.920
so they essentially just copied A New Hope. Were&nbsp;
at a point where people are starting to despise&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:37.920 --> 00:18:42.200
the two types of content that Disney Plus has&nbsp;
to offer, and this goes into my final point&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:42.200 --> 00:18:49.000
for Disney Plus’ overall issues. Which is simply,&nbsp;
Disney Plus only has Marvel and Star Wars content.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:49.000 --> 00:18:53.560
They basically have no other high quality and&nbsp;
sought after original shows with unique stories&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:53.560 --> 00:18:58.840
that people want to watch. When you look at every&nbsp;
other platform, their most popular show tends to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:58.840 --> 00:19:04.080
be an original series that balloons into having&nbsp;
immense popularity. Like Netflix having Stranger&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:04.080 --> 00:19:10.000
Things, Apple having Ted Lasso or Severance,&nbsp;
Amazon Prime having The Boys, and really any&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:10.000 --> 00:19:15.360
show on HBO at this point. But for Disney Plus?&nbsp;
They seem to be putting all of their eggs into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:15.360 --> 00:19:21.640
two baskets and even shows that they do make like&nbsp;
Willow, that aren’t based on Marvel or Star Wars,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:21.640 --> 00:19:26.320
they instantly cancel and immediately remove off&nbsp;
of their streaming service as if they were deeply&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:26.320 --> 00:19:30.720
embarrassed by it. Not saying that that show&nbsp;
was good or anything, I don’t think anyone even&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:30.720 --> 00:19:35.720
watched that show or wanted it. It just seems like&nbsp;
a baffling decision to give an obscure and niche&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:35.720 --> 00:19:42.040
IP its own show with a $100 million dollar budget.&nbsp;
Again going back to them probably needing to make&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:42.040 --> 00:19:47.760
pilots for their shows and having higher quality&nbsp;
assurance. Currently, the only big show coming&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:47.760 --> 00:19:52.800
out on Disney Plus that isn’t Marvel or Star Wars&nbsp;
related is another adaptation of Percy Jackson and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:52.800 --> 00:19:57.920
the Olympians. I hope this show is good, but given&nbsp;
that Disney Plus has garnered a bad reputation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:57.920 --> 00:20:04.000
for low quality shows, I’m not exactly excited for&nbsp;
this series when normally I would be. It’s kind of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:04.000 --> 00:20:09.200
sad, but you can only disappoint fans for so long&nbsp;
until they cut ties to your streaming service.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:09.200 --> 00:20:14.600
Which is pretty surprising considering that Disney&nbsp;
Plus has really only been around for a few years.

00:20:14.600 --> 00:20:19.320
So I don’t necessarily want to end this video&nbsp;
on a depressing and gloomy note of Disney Plus&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:19.320 --> 00:20:24.080
and the content they're making being terrible&nbsp;
because at least on the side of MCU content,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:24.080 --> 00:20:28.440
they are making strides to do better. They’re&nbsp;
going to start hiring showrunners for upcoming&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:28.440 --> 00:20:32.960
shows who have a more consistent and creative&nbsp;
vision so that there isn’t a constant frenzy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:32.960 --> 00:20:37.240
behind the scenes. They’re going to start hiring&nbsp;
more TV executives so that they aren’t stretched&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:37.240 --> 00:20:42.160
thin between movies and shows. It’s rumored that&nbsp;
Kevin Feige is going to start hiring more tried&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:42.160 --> 00:20:47.840
and tested talent instead of relative unknowns.&nbsp;
Mainly because by hiring new directors there’s a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:47.840 --> 00:20:52.440
bit of uncertainty as to whether they will perform&nbsp;
well, so they end up needing to be supervised&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:52.440 --> 00:20:57.480
more. They’ve also seemed to be hiring more&nbsp;
experienced writers like Nic Pizzolato for Blade&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:57.480 --> 00:21:02.960
who was the writer for True Detective and Lee&nbsp;
Sung Jin for Thunderbolts who wrote Beef. So they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:02.960 --> 00:21:07.960
probably won’t take another chance on someone like&nbsp;
Jeff Loveness again who wrote Ant-Man Quantumania.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:07.960 --> 00:21:12.840
Because prior to that his primary experience&nbsp;
was writing some Rick and Morty episodes. Then&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:12.840 --> 00:21:17.320
finally they are going to start focusing on more&nbsp;
serialized TV. This means shows will be geared&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:17.320 --> 00:21:21.440
towards multiple seasons instead of mini-series&nbsp;
that are unsure about whether or not they’re&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:21.440 --> 00:21:26.560
getting another season. So these four changes will&nbsp;
hopefully lead to better and more fine-tuned shows&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:26.560 --> 00:21:31.440
on Disney Plus. The writing itself will still&nbsp;
be a major indicator for quality obviously,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:31.440 --> 00:21:36.080
but at least the environment for making&nbsp;
this content won’t be so hospitable. Also,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:36.080 --> 00:21:41.080
another major change coming to Disney Plus is that&nbsp;
it’s going to be combined with Hulu in an attempt&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:41.080 --> 00:21:46.200
to increase the value of the overall package. The&nbsp;
main reason this is happening is because Disney&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:46.200 --> 00:21:51.800
Plus hasn’t made any money since it started. It’s&nbsp;
been operating at a constant loss every quarter,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:51.800 --> 00:21:57.520
some of which Disney has lost 500 Million to even&nbsp;
a billion dollars. This was honestly a pretty&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:57.520 --> 00:22:02.440
major revelation to me and it also seems that the&nbsp;
service is starting to go down a spiral in that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:02.440 --> 00:22:08.160
for the last two quarters they reported losses&nbsp;
for subscribers. Disney Plus does have over&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:08.160 --> 00:22:12.920
146 million subscribers which is the third&nbsp;
highest so It’s kind of surprising that they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:12.920 --> 00:22:17.720
are losing this much money. Probably points to&nbsp;
the behind-the-scenes of the service being even&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:17.720 --> 00:22:22.040
worse than what we just went over in this video.&nbsp;
So yeah, I’ll end it here since the video is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:22.040 --> 00:22:26.960
getting really long and I didn’t want to go on an&nbsp;
unhinged MCU rant and wanted to provide some kind&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:26.960 --> 00:22:31.800
of new perspective. So hopefully this video was&nbsp;
informative in some way. Thank you for watching&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:31.800 --> 00:22:44.840
and check out my socials for Letterboxd,&nbsp;
Discord, and Patreon in the description.

